EN = [
 ('dig1', 'One last sweep of the brush, and what it uncovers is the brush itself, and the trowel, set down.'),
 ('dig2', 'Forty-three episodes ago we started with the question of how anybody knows anything about a period with no newspapers, no archives and no surviving eyewitness. This episode is the answer, walked through one case at a time.'),
 ('src1', "All five, for the only time in the series. Things dug up. Texts. Inscriptions. Coins. Travellers' accounts. No single one of them would be enough. Every episode in this series has been an exercise in putting at least two of them against each other."),
 ('frz1', 'The first room. A hand axe, and the long prehistory that produced it. A painted pot, and the first farmers. Then the Harappan cases: the brick and the drain, the seal, the bronze figurine — a civilisation of cities we can see in detail and cannot read a word of.'),
 ('frz2', 'Then the Vedic rooms: a palm-leaf manuscript, painted grey ware, a fire altar, and a burial stone from the megalithic south.'),
 ('frz3', 'The second stretch. A punch-marked coin and a city wall, as cities and money return together after a thousand years. A tree above an empty throne; a stupa; a votive tablet — the age of the Buddha and Mahavira, shown as that age showed itself.'),
 ('frz4', 'A Greek-style coin, for the arrivals from the north-west. And then the Mauryas: a manual of statecraft, a bull capital, a rock edict, a polished pillar.'),
 ('frz5', 'The third stretch. A carved gateway, and the merchants and monks who paid for it. Coins in two scripts, then Kushana gold, as the north-west becomes a crossroads. A hero stone and a Tamil manuscript, for the south.'),
 ('frz6', 'A Roman amphora, carried thousands of miles and thrown away here. Then Gupta gold, a copper plate, an iron pillar that will not rust — and Kailasa, a temple made by taking a hillside away.'),
 ('frz7', 'And the last stretch. A Chola temple tower and a state that wrote its accounts on walls. A star-shaped plan. A hill fort.'),
 ('frz8', "Then the thematic cases: a zero, a monastery seal, a traveller's staff, a boundary stone, a tray of coins, and a tablet of women's names. Forty-three cases, and the hall is full."),
 ('plq1', 'One last disagreement, and it is about where we stopped. The conventional endpoint is around 1200 CE. But periodisation is a convenience imposed afterwards: regional states, land grants and temple building were well under way centuries before, and nothing about ordinary life changed in a single year.'),
 ('plq2', 'The three-part division of Indian history into ancient, medieval and modern was itself proposed in the colonial period on religious lines, and historians have largely moved away from that framing. Know the dates the syllabus uses, and know that they are a filing system, not a fact.'),
 ('ins1', 'Here is the last memory tip, carved in stone. The whole series in seven words. Stone, seed, city, state, faith, empire, region.'),
 ('quiz1', 'A last Dig Quiz, and these three are about method. First: the script we can see clearly on thousands of objects and still cannot read. The Harappan script.'),
 ('quiz2', "Second: the kind of document that gives us a ruler's own words, dated and placed. An inscription. Third: the class of document on which the whole feudalism debate rests."),
 ('quiz3', 'Land grants. In Mains, the examiner rewards one habit above all: naming your source and saying what kind of source it is.'),
 ('quiz4', '"The Arthashastra says" and "the inscription records" are not the same claim, and showing that you know the difference is most of the marks.'),
 ('gal1', 'The lamps come up on the whole hall at last. Forty-four cases, seven rooms, and about two and a half million years, from a stone tool to a temple cut out of a hillside.'),
 ('gal2', "Everything in here rests on something somebody dug up, wrote down, carved or buried — and where the evidence runs out, this series has tried to say so rather than fill the gap. Thank you for walking it with us. Next: Medieval India, Simply Painted. To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios."),
]

